[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you picked up a customizable Live2D model and have no clue where or how to start using it, or maybe you\'re just interested in getting one but don\'t know what\'s involved with using them, we\'re going to go over all the basics like where to get one, pricing, VTube Studio tips, and even capturing your model'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inside of OBS. Timestamps will be below if you\'re looking for something in particular, but let\'s get started. If you\'re watching this video out of curiosity on how customizable models work, let me clarify some info for you. Otherwise, skip ahead. Basically, these'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'models come with several hairstyle, clothing, and color options built into the model, which you can choose from using the parameters that the rigger put into the model. Because they offer many options without needing to'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'professionally consult and order something custom, they\'re usually offered for a fraction of the cost of a normal model. Generally speaking, priced between 20 and $200, whereas [music] a full-on customized model might be reaching 500 to over 12,000. But, let\'s'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get started by actually picking up one of these. The most popular place to pick one up is going to be VGen or Booth. These are both online marketplaces for artists and creatives. Typically, you can find them by searching for'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'customizable model, and in VGen, just make sure to swap to the shop tab before searching. The model that I will be using today is called the Light Street Girl model by Kioki Studio. As you can see, it retails for about $24 USD, but it does have more limited options'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compared to some of the more robust models like the Suika or Twintails model. And those cost a bit more due to the additional options that they offer. Most will include a grid of options or a YouTube video displaying all of them.'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Look out for which model has components that best represent you, your VTuber concept, or your established design if you have one already. Be sure to scan for textured hairstyles and black hairstyles as well, and look for things like separated palm art mesh for deeper'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'skin tones, as not all models offer these options. Most models will list the available expressions or toggles on the model like sticker expressions or animations like waving. Also, be on the lookout for body type customization as'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well, including things like chest size, tummy adjustments, or body weight. And if your design has major components like a tail, ears, or horns, just make sure that the model has something similar. Some models do include special editions'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like Vbridger rigging, which will make your mouth tracking look more accurate, or other things like controller tracking as well. Some additions, most notably Vbridger, require a plugin that will incur additional costs onto the model.'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, be sure to consider that before purchasing. Whichever model you decide to purchase, once you buy it, you should get an instant download. If you don\'t download right away though, you can always access the files by navigating to your library in either booth or VGen and'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'downloading them from there. Models typically come with an assistive YouTube video or a readme/user tutorial file. So, be sure to check for those in case there\'s any model-specific information that you might need to read. Your'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'download will contain a zip file which includes your model folder. Unzip the folder to get the model folder, and then we can go ahead and open VTube Studio. If you don\'t have VTube Studio yet, you can pick it up on Steam for free and install it from there. And once you have'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it, go ahead and launch it. Inside of VTube Studio, double-click and go to the model selection tab. Click on import your own model and select the open folder option, and it will pull up the folder where all of your model information is stored. Drag your model'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'folder into this folder and be sure to drag the folder itself, not just the files inside. After that, search up for your model inside of VTube Studio. If it doesn\'t show up, click on the model button a few times until it populates,'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then select your model. If you buy a pre-made model, you should not be prompted to do auto setup because the rigger should have set it up for you. But if you happen to get this setup window, simply run auto setup for it. This generates a ton of default tracking'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'parameters, but like I said, it should be set up by your model maker, so potentially it\'s a mistake if you see this prompt when you open up the model. I would double-check your model instructions just to make sure you\'re not missing something. When you first load your model in, your model is not'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to be moving because you have to link up your tracking and calibrate parameters to your face. That\'s a pretty in-depth discussion since there are multiple different types of tracking and the calibration process can be pretty confusing for most people, especially'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'beginners. If you don\'t know how to do this, please check out these sections of my VTube Studio crash course and get your model tracking set up and ready to go. Once you\'re calibrated and have tracking ready to go though, we can finally customize things. To do so, open'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your settings and head to the expressions tab and then make a new expression. Name it whatever you see fit and then go through all of the parameters, toggle them on and adjust the values to cycle through different'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'options on the model. Most models will have clothing options, hair options and some will have color sliders as well. But if your model doesn\'t have color sliders, don\'t panic because there\'s still another way that you can recolor your model. When the model is looking'

[04:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how you want it to, be sure to toggle on this customization expression option. This will give it a star symbol next to it and will prevent [music] this toggle you made from turning off when you\'re removing your other expressions. Then click finish. on the expression, you can'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go into the expressions editor menu and select turn on/off expressions. [music] Then just find your new expression and turn it on and your customization will now be active. Now, even if you have other toggles on and you clear them all,'

[05:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this expression will always remain in the background. It should be noted though that every time you open VTube Studio, you\'ll have to reactivate this expression, but you can prevent this by going to the model settings tab and toggling on the option to save active'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'expressions. It will keep all expressions active on app launch. But this includes everything, not just defaults. So if you leave a bunch of miscellaneous expressions on when you close, those will also be there. If you'

[05:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to edit colors but you don\'t have any color sliders on your model, in your model settings tab, you can select customize model and choose the multiply/screen color art mesh option. Here you can select individual art mesh'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and adjust both the darkening and lightening color. This takes an extremely long time to do though because you probably have tons of art mesh on your model. So I recommend using the from group option at the top. You can select a group and recolor multiple art'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mesh simultaneously. But, if no groups are listed here, you\'ll need to close out the editor and customize your model again. Except this time, select the set up layer groups option. Add a new group name in, and then go through and select'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'each art mesh that you would like to include in this group. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; You can do so using tags in the user data file information, or by using the art mesh name as well. And once you\'re done, save the group, return to the art mesh editor, and now it should populate'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the group list. The group feature is relatively new, so not all models will have groups pre-made on them. Additionally, if you want further customization, you can actually open the model folder, find your textures, and edit them inside of an art application'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like Clip Studio Paint or Photoshop. You would find the texture that includes the component that you want to edit, open the file up in your editing app, and make sure to stay inside the bounds of that original art mesh shape by using clipping masks. When you\'re done'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'editing, you can re-export the texture to the same file name. Because this will overwrite the file name though, make sure to create backups of the original textures just in case. With your model tracking setup and customization ready'

[06:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to go, all you need to do is add it into OBS. First, let\'s toggle on transparency. Double-click and go to your background options, and then select the color picker background. Set it to black so that none of the background shows. Then, toggle on transparent'

[07:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'capture. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Next, inside of the webcam tracking menu, scroll down and toggle on Spout 2. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Then, click on the info section and click the more info link. This should open up the Spout 2 OBS plugin page, complete with a full tutorial here. But,'

[07:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'basically, all you have to do is close OBS, download the installer, and run it. And if you don\'t use the installer, you can also add the Win Spout folder to your OBS Studio plugins folder manually. When you start OBS, you should now be'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'able to add a new source type called Spout 2. Add one and set the first option to be first sender or VTube Studio Spout. [music] Use fast capture, and I recommend using pre-multiplied alpha as your capture type. You should now be able to use backgrounds inside of'

[07:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'OBS without your model covering things up. If you want more info on how to add hot keys and Twitch integration into your VTube model, I recommend checking out my VTube Studio crash course or my guide to Streamer Bot integration. So, be sure to check those out next. [music]'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Thank you guys for watching and I will see you in the next one. Bye. &gt;&gt; [music]'
